# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# JavaScript: an audited repository's lockfile is its own; installed packages
# are never committed here.
node_modules/

# Virtual environment (this project uses exactly one: .venv)
.venv/

# Local settings (copy .env.example to .env)
.env

# Tests
.pytest_cache/

# Audited code is never committed: the auditor is pointed at a repository by
# path or URL, and this repository holds no other project's source. What it
# does commit is grading_keys/ -- this project's own hand-written keys, pins
# and baselines about apps -- so that folder is deliberately NOT ignored.

# Repositories fetched by src/fetch_repo.py, and the pins written beside them.
# The pin is ignored too, deliberately: it is generated output about an
# arbitrary repository someone fetched once. Adopting a fetched tree as a
# graded app means transcribing its pin into grading_keys/ by hand, so exactly
# one committed pin per app exists and two copies can never disagree.
fetched/

# The knowledge base the remediation advice is grounded on: an upstream clone
# per source and the ChromaDB index built from it, both fetched and built
# out-of-band (see the README). Only the manifest that pins them is committed:
# the ignore covers the folder's contents and re-admits the two tracked files.
knowledge/*
!knowledge/manifest.json
!knowledge/README.md

# Generated output (surfaces.json, sbom.json, mapping.json, ...). Anchored with
# a leading slash on purpose: a bare `artifacts/` also matches src/artifacts/
# and tests/artifacts/, which silently made those source files unstageable.
/artifacts/

# Local model weights — never commit these
*.gguf
*.bin
*.safetensors

# Task 4.9, decided: docs/REPORT.md is tracked -- a diffable source is worth
# more to a reviewer than a Word file -- and the stale report.docx is dropped
# along with build_report.py, the helper that built it.

# AI / agent tooling (local only — not part of the submitted project)
.claude/
CLAUDE.md
